        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="why-valve-gaskets-matter">Why Valve Gaskets Matter in Aerosol Systems</h2>
        </div>
        <p>In an aerosol container, the gasket forms the primary seal between the valve mounting cup, valve stem, and can rim. It must maintain integrity under internal pressure while remaining chemically inert to the formulation and propellant throughout the product’s shelf life. Any degradation directly compromises safety, spray performance, and storage stability.</p>
        <p>Unlike many other components, the gasket is in continuous contact with both liquid product and vapor-phase propellant. This dual exposure creates complex chemical and mechanical stresses that cannot be addressed through generic rubber selection.</p>
        <p>Key implications of gasket performance include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Long-term pressure retention and leak prevention</li>
            <li>Consistent spray rate and actuation force</li>
            <li>Shelf-life stability across temperature cycles</li>
            <li>Safety during storage, transport, and consumer use</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="how-gaskets-function">How Aerosol Valve Gaskets Function</h2>
        </div>
        <p>The gasket works by elastic compression rather than rigid sealing. When the valve is crimped onto the can, the gasket is compressed between metal surfaces to create a hermetic seal. During actuation, the gasket must flex repeatedly without losing resilience or dimensional stability.</p>
        <p>This repetitive compression and relaxation exposes the gasket to mechanical fatigue. Simultaneously, chemical exposure can cause swelling, hardening, or extraction of plasticizers, which permanently alters gasket behavior.</p>
        <p>Functional requirements of a valve gasket include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>High elastic recovery after compression</li>
            <li>Controlled compression set under constant load</li>
            <li>Resistance to chemical swelling and shrinkage</li>
            <li>Stability across operating temperature ranges</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="fundamentals-chemistry">Fundamentals of Valve Gasket Chemistry</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Valve gaskets are manufactured from elastomers—polymers designed to exhibit rubber-like elasticity. Compatibility is not defined by whether a gasket dissolves, but by how its physical properties change over time when exposed to specific chemicals.</p>
        <p>Chemical incompatibility typically manifests as swelling, embrittlement, softening, or loss of elastic memory. These changes are driven by molecular-level interactions between the elastomer polymer chains and solvents, propellants, oils, or additives in the formulation.</p>
        <p>Critical chemical interaction mechanisms include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Solvent diffusion into the elastomer matrix</li>
            <li>Plasticizer extraction from rubber compounds</li>
            <li>Polymer chain scission or crosslink disruption</li>
            <li>Oxidative or thermal degradation</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="common-materials">Common Aerosol Valve Gasket Materials</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Different elastomers are used in aerosol valves based on formulation chemistry, propellant system, and target market requirements. Each material offers predictable strengths and limitations derived from its polymer backbone.</p>
        <h3>NBR (Nitrile Rubber / Buna-N)</h3>
        <p>NBR is widely used in aerosol valves due to its good resistance to aliphatic hydrocarbons and oils. It performs reliably in solvent-based systems where polar solvents are limited.</p>
        <p>Typical characteristics of NBR gaskets:</p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Characteristic</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Good compatibility with LPG propellants</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Moderate resistance to oils and greases</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Poor resistance to water, alcohols, and ketones</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Limited high-temperature performance</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>EPDM (Ethylene Propylene Diene Monomer)</h3>
        <p>EPDM is selected for water-based and polar formulations. Its saturated polymer backbone offers excellent resistance to water, steam, and many detergents but poor resistance to hydrocarbons.</p>
        <p>Key EPDM properties include:</p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Property</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Excellent water and alcohol resistance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Very good ozone and heat resistance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Incompatible with mineral oils and LPG</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Common in cosmetic and household aerosols</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Neoprene (Chloroprene Rubber)</h3>
        <p>Neoprene provides balanced performance across moderate chemical exposures. It is often used where neither extreme hydrocarbon nor extreme water resistance is required.</p>
        <p>Performance characteristics of neoprene gaskets:</p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Characteristic</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Moderate resistance to oils and solvents</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Better weathering resistance than NBR</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Limited compatibility with aggressive solvents</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stable mechanical properties under cycling</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Butyl Rubber (IIR)</h3>
        <p>Butyl rubber is valued for its extremely low gas permeability. It is used when vapor loss and pressure retention are critical over long storage periods.</p>
        <p>Butyl gasket attributes include:</p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Attribute</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Excellent gas impermeability</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Good resistance to acids and alkalis</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Limited solvent compatibility</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Lower elastic recovery compared to NBR</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Fluoroelastomers (FKM / Viton®)</h3>
        <p>FKM gaskets represent the highest chemical resistance class used in aerosols. Their fluorinated polymer structure resists aggressive solvents, oils, and high temperatures.</p>
        <p>Characteristics of FKM gaskets:</p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Characteristic</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Exceptional chemical and solvent resistance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>High-temperature stability</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Low swelling across wide formulations</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Higher material and processing cost</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
